---
/**
 * The front page, in the Pehmeä direction: rounded cards on a blush page, a hand
 * holding the phone on a coral disc, and the app's own card tints carrying the loop.
 *
 * Two photographs, each a stock photo with a real still of the app set into its
 * phone: the home screen in the hero, cut out of its photograph, and an invitation
 * received in the footer's beta card, which only this page hands a photograph. A
 * still in a photograph goes stale with the app like any other, so
 * `docs/pictures.md` accounts for them. Every other screen here is the app's own
 * still in a `Phone` frame.
 *
 * The order is the argument, and it is the owner's pitch: what the other apps make
 * you do, struck through in a line of steps under the hero, what Pilke does instead
 * in four steps, why its candidates and its dates can be trusted, and who would be
 * better off elsewhere. Each answers why anybody should care before the next one is
 * asked, so the page reads as one case top to bottom and the waitlist under it is
 * where the case ends.
 *
 * The page says each thing once and points to where it is said in full: the loop to
 * `nain-se-toimii`, safety to `turvallisuus`, the rest of the questions to
 * `kysyttya`. Sparks are not here at all: they are how an invitation is paid for,
 * which matters once somebody is using the app. A front page that explains
 * everything is a page nobody reads to the end.
 *
 * One page at every width. The 1440 artboard and the 390 artboard are the two ends
 * of it, and every size in between is a straight line from one to the other; where
 * a layout changes rather than scales — the hero's phone moving under the copy, the
 * steps going from one column to two, the quality and safety cards going side by
 * side — it changes at one of four lines, 40rem,
 * 64rem, 69rem and 75rem, so the page has a few shapes and not a dozen.
 *
 * The waitlist is not on this page. It is the footer, in `SiteFooter.astro`, so that
 * a reader who came to `turvallisuus` can join from where they are; the hero's button
 * and the header's are anchors down to it.
 */
import { Picture } from 'astro:assets';
import betaEn from '../../assets/photos/beta-invitation-en.jpg';
import betaFi from '../../assets/photos/beta-invitation-fi.jpg';
import heroEn from '../../assets/photos/hero-cutout-en.png';
import heroFi from '../../assets/photos/hero-cutout-fi.png';
import heroPhoneMask from '../../assets/photos/hero-cutout-phone.png';
import { shotsFor } from '../../assets/screens';
import SparkleScatter from '../../components/SparkleScatter.astro';
import Phone from '../../components/Phone.astro';
import { localeOf, localePath, localeRoutes, useTranslations } from '../../i18n/ui';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

export const getStaticPaths = () => localeRoutes();

const lang = localeOf(Astro.params);
const shots = shotsFor(lang);
const t = useTranslations(lang);

/*
  Each photograph carries a still of the app, so each is made once per language
  and the page draws the one in its own: the English page shows the English app.
*/
/*
  The word the English headline underlines. "Treffisovellus" is a joke in Finnish
  that "dating app" is not, so the owner asked for *dating* to carry the stress in
  English. Fredoka has no italic, so it is a coral underline, the strikes' bar.
  Finnish underlines nothing. A title that no longer holds the word is drawn plain.
*/
const heroMark = { fi: null, en: 'dating' }[lang];
const heroTitle = t('hero.title');
const heroSplit = heroMark === null ? -1 : heroTitle.indexOf(heroMark);

const photos = {
  fi: { hero: heroFi, beta: betaFi },
  en: { hero: heroEn, beta: betaEn },
}[lang];

/*
  The other apps' routine, a step at a time, as the lines between the hero and the
  Pilke section strike it through. "Swaippaa" twice, because it comes round again.
*/
const elsewhereSteps = [
  t('pitch.elsewhere.swipe'),
  t('pitch.elsewhere.chat'),
  t('pitch.elsewhere.ghost'),
  t('pitch.elsewhere.swipe'),
  t('pitch.elsewhere.more'),
  t('pitch.elsewhere.maybe'),
];

/*
  What Pilke does instead, in the owner's four steps, each on one of the app's four
  card tints: an invitation received is pink, a confirmed date peach and the
  feedback after it yellow, and your wishes take the blue, the one left, so the four
  are the app's whole set. The tilt alternates so the four phones do not read as a
  stamped row.

  Each screen is the step being done: the Mieltymykset questions a reader filters
  by, with two answered, an invitation received, the date with its map, and the
  feedback's facets with a few picked. Mieltymykset carries the owner's *Tulossa
  pian* chip, laid over its phone in the page rather than drawn into the still, so
  it goes when the set ships without a retake; it is text in the card, so a screen
  reader reads it with the step.
*/
const steps = [
  { title: t('pitch.how.wishes.title'), body: t('pitch.how.wishes.body'), shot: shots.mieltymykset, alt: t('shot.mieltymykset'), tint: 'var(--card-sent)', soon: t('pitch.how.wishes.soon') },
  { title: t('pitch.how.invite.title'), body: t('pitch.how.invite.body'), shot: shots.invitation, alt: t('shot.invitation'), tint: 'var(--card-received)' },
  { title: t('pitch.how.date.title'), body: t('pitch.how.date.body'), shot: shots.date, alt: t('shot.date'), tint: 'var(--card-confirmed)' },
  { title: t('pitch.how.after.title'), body: t('pitch.how.after.body'), shot: shots.feedback, alt: t('shot.feedback'), tint: 'var(--card-feedback)' },
];

/*
  Who would be better off with another app, and which. The owner's own four, with
  the competitors named: the joke is that Pilke says so itself, and it only works
  if the answer is a real app somebody could go and download.
*/
const notFor = [
  { q: t('pitch.notfor.more.q'), a: t('pitch.notfor.more.a') },
  { q: t('pitch.notfor.chat.q'), a: t('pitch.notfor.chat.a') },
  { q: t('pitch.notfor.phone.q'), a: t('pitch.notfor.phone.a') },
  { q: t('pitch.notfor.algorithm.q'), a: t('pitch.notfor.algorithm.a') },
];
---

<Base lang={lang} betaPhoto={{ src: photos.beta, alt: t('photo.beta') }}>
  <section>
    <div class="wrap hero">
      <SparkleScatter
        fill="var(--sparkle-on-peach)"
        fold="hero"
        sparkles={[
          { size: 100, rotate: 14, left: '36%', top: '40px', only: 'wide' },
          { size: 38, rotate: -26, left: 'calc(36% + 116px)', top: '120px', only: 'wide' },
          { size: 132, rotate: -18, left: '40px', bottom: '30px', only: 'wide' },
          { size: 44, rotate: 30, left: '190px', bottom: '130px', only: 'wide' },
          { size: 76, rotate: 36, left: '41.5%', bottom: '90px', only: 'wide' },
          { size: 90, rotate: 10, right: '40px', top: '30px', only: 'wide' },
          { size: 34, rotate: -32, right: '150px', top: '56px', only: 'wide' },
          { size: 66, rotate: -38, right: '40px', bottom: '30px', only: 'wide' },
          { size: 60, rotate: 14, right: '16px', top: '12px', only: 'narrow' },
          { size: 26, rotate: -28, right: '84px', top: '22px', only: 'narrow' },
          { size: 52, rotate: 38, right: '16px', bottom: '16px', only: 'narrow' },
        ]}
      />

      <div class="hero-copy">
        <h1>
          {heroSplit < 0 || heroMark === null
            ? heroTitle
            : <Fragment>{heroTitle.slice(0, heroSplit)}<span class="hero-mark">{heroMark}</span>{heroTitle.slice(heroSplit + heroMark.length)}</Fragment>}
        </h1>
        <p class="lede">{t('pitch.hero.lead')}</p>
        <!--
          The footer is the form, so the hero points at it rather than carrying a
          second copy. Two forms on a page are two things to wire up and two places
          to disagree about what was submitted.
        -->
        <p class="actions">
          <a class="go" href="#beta">{t('cta.button')}</a>
        </p>
      </div>

      <!--
        A hand holding the phone, cut out of its photograph and standing on a coral
        disc. The disc is the brand's colour at full strength and the one place on
        the first screen it is; the screen in the hand is the app's home, so that is
        what the eye lands on. The cut-out is transparent, served as AVIF or WebP
        with the PNG as the fallback. `scripts/photos/cutout.py` makes it.

        The disc clips the hand and the arm: they end on its outline, crisp. The
        phone stands out over the disc's edge at its top and right, with the thumb
        and the fingers gripping it. Both are one mask on the one picture, the disc's
        circle and the phone's part (`hero-cutout-phone.png`, the same size: the
        phone's body and the gripping fingers, each by its own contour) added
        together, so the outline is only ever the disc's arc, the phone's edge or a
        finger's, and nothing is drawn twice.

        Drawn 118.9% of the art box's width, most of the overhang the arm running out
        left of the disc, which the disc clips: 642px beside the copy, and at most
        476px stacked; the widths are those at 1x and 2x.
      -->
      <div class="hero-art" style={`--phone-mask: url(${heroPhoneMask.src})`}>
        <div class="disc" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <Picture
          src={photos.hero}
          formats={['avif', 'webp']}
          alt={t('photo.hero')}
          widths={[480, 642, 960, 1284, 1400]}
          sizes="(min-width: 69rem) 642px, min(106vw, 476px)"
          loading="eager"
          class="hero-cut"
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!--
    The other apps' routine, struck through, between the hero and the Pilke section:
    the bridge from "finally, a dating app" to what Pilke does instead. No card of
    its own, on the page's ground, so it reads as the passage from one card to the
    next rather than as a section.

    Each step is a `<del>`, which is what struck-through text is, with a real space
    after it so the steps are words apart when read aloud or copied, and a hidden
    lead-in says they are struck to a screen reader, since most of them read
    `<del>` as plain text. The heading under them, "Pilkkeessä ei swaippailla eikä
    chättäillä", is the answer to these lines.
  -->
  <section class="slab slab-open elsewhere" aria-labelledby="elsewhere-note">
    <p class="wrap">
      <span class="visually-hidden" id="elsewhere-note">{t('pitch.elsewhere.note')}</span>
      {elsewhereSteps.map((step) => <Fragment><del>{step}</del>{' '}</Fragment>)}
    </p>
  </section>

  <!--
    What Pilke does instead, and how it goes. The heading is the owner's own
    contrast line, read straight after the struck-through steps, and the lead turns
    it into the four steps. On the page's own ground, so the four tinted cards are
    the section and nothing frames them twice; the heading and its lead are
    centred over the cards.

    The phones run off the bottom of their cards on purpose: the top of a screen
    is what says which screen it is, and a whole phone in each of four cards made
    them four posters. The card clips its phone; inside the phone the screen runs
    edge to edge.
  -->
  <section class="slab slab-open how" id="how" aria-labelledby="how-title">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="how-head">
        <h2 class="display-mid" id="how-title">{t('pitch.instead.title')}</h2>
        <p class="lede">{t('pitch.how.lead')}</p>
      </div>

      <ol class="steps">
        {
          steps.map((step, i) => (
            <li style={`background:${step.tint}`}>
              <div class="step-copy">
                <span class="numeral" aria-hidden="true">{i + 1}</span>
                <h3>{step.title}</h3>
                <p>{step.body}</p>
              </div>
              <div class="step-phone">
                <div class="phone-hold">
                  <Phone src={step.shot} alt={step.alt} width={230} tilt={i % 2 === 0 ? 3 : -3} />
                  {step.soon && <span class="tag soon">{step.soon}</span>}
                </div>
              </div>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>

      <p class="how-more">
        <a class="go" href={localePath(lang, 'nain-se-toimii')}>{t('pitch.instead.more')}</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!--
    Why the candidates and the dates can be trusted, the owner's two cards, side by
    side once the row has room for both and stacked before that. Each is a slab of
    its own on one of the site's grounds, peach and the safety page's mint, and the
    pair is laid out with the page grid's own gap, so the two stand as far apart as
    any two sections do.
  -->
  <div class="pair">
    <section class="slab slab-pale" aria-labelledby="quality-title">
      <div class="wrap section-pad pair-card">
        <h2 class="display-mid" id="quality-title">{t('pitch.quality.title')}</h2>
        <p>{t('pitch.quality.body')}</p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="slab slab-mint" aria-labelledby="safety-title">
      <div class="wrap section-pad pair-card">
        <h2 class="display-mid" id="safety-title">{t('pitch.safety.title')}</h2>
        <p>{t('pitch.safety.body')}</p>
        <p class="pair-more">
          <a class="go" href={localePath(lang, 'turvallisuus')}>{t('safety.more')}</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <!--
    Who Pilke is not for, last before the waitlist: a reader who has come this far
    and still wants to swipe is told where to, kindly, and everybody else is one
    card from joining. The question is the big line, because it is the reader's own
    doubt in their own words, and the answer under it is said quietly, as a tip.
  -->
  <section class="slab slab-white not-for" aria-labelledby="notfor-title">
    <div class="wrap section-pad">
      <h2 class="display-mid" id="notfor-title">{t('pitch.notfor.title')}</h2>
      <p class="lede">{t('pitch.notfor.lead')}</p>
      <dl class="qas">
        {
          notFor.map((item) => (
            <div class="qa">
              <dt>{item.q}</dt>
              <dd>{item.a}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>
      <p class="not-for-more">
        <a class="go" href={localePath(lang, 'kysyttya')}>{t('pitch.notfor.questions')}</a>
        <a class="go ghost" href={localePath(lang, 'yhteystiedot')}>{t('pitch.notfor.contact')}</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  /* ─── Hero ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

  .hero {
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
    isolation: isolate;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 20px;
    padding: 28px 22px;
    background: var(--brand-background);
    border-radius: clamp(36px, 1.14vw + 31.5px, 48px);
  }

  /* A size container, so the headline can be set in the column's own width: the
     compound in the Finnish headline is the longest word on the site, and a size
     that fits the column is the only one that never overflows it. */
  .hero-copy {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: clamp(20px, 0.76vw + 17px, 28px);
    container-type: inline-size;
  }

  /* 13.8cqi because the compound is 7.1em wide in Fredoka: at that size it fits the
     column whole, and only a phone narrower than the artboard's breaks it at its
     soft hyphen. */
  h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(2.75rem, 13.8cqi, 5.5rem);
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    hyphens: manual;
  }

  .hero .lede {
    max-width: 500px;
  }

  .actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 10px;
  }

  .actions .go {
    min-height: clamp(56px, 0.38vw + 54.5px, 60px);
    padding-inline: 32px;
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
  }

  /*
    The hand and phone on the disc, drawn in fractions of the art box so it is one
    picture at any size, the same at every width. The disc is the box's width; the
    cut-out is 118.89% of it, from 22.89% out past the box's left, and the disc's
    centre is 63.52% below the cut-out's reference line, `--cut-top`; the image
    starts 0.65% above that line, room for the phone's silver frame. `scripts/photos/cutout.py` bakes the
    fingers' part of the mask against that same circle, so the numbers move
    together or not at all.

    The mask is the disc's own circle, in the cut-out's coordinates: its centre is
    the disc's centre less the cut-out's offset, and its radius the disc's, half a
    pixel wider at the edge so the hand covers the disc's anti-aliased rim and no
    coral shows between them. Added to it is the phone's body.
  */
  .hero-art {
    position: relative;
    width: min(100%, 400px);
    aspect-ratio: 100 / 124;
    margin: 8px auto 0;
    container-type: inline-size;
    --cut-top: 4cqi;
    --disc-y: calc(var(--cut-top) + 63.52cqi);
  }

  .disc {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: var(--disc-y);
    width: 100cqi;
    aspect-ratio: 1;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--brand-main);
    transform: translateY(-50%);
  }

  .hero-art :global(.hero-cut) {
    --circle: radial-gradient(
      circle 50cqi at 72.89cqi 64.17cqi,
      #000 50cqi,
      transparent calc(50cqi + 1px)
    );
    position: absolute;
    left: -22.89cqi;
    top: calc(var(--cut-top) - 0.65cqi);
    width: 118.89cqi;
    max-width: none;
    height: auto;
    -webkit-mask-image: var(--circle), var(--phone-mask);
    mask-image: var(--circle), var(--phone-mask);
    -webkit-mask-size: 100% 100%, 100% 100%;
    mask-size: 100% 100%, 100% 100%;
    -webkit-mask-repeat: no-repeat;
    mask-repeat: no-repeat;
    -webkit-mask-composite: source-over;
    mask-composite: add;
  }

  @media (min-width: 40rem) {
    .actions {
      flex-direction: row;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      gap: 12px;
    }
  }

  /* Stacked on a tablet the headline has the card's whole width, and a line of it
     runs under the sparkle in the top corner. It stops short of the sparkle instead;
     the size stays the column's, so only where a line breaks changes. */
  @media (min-width: 40rem) and (max-width: 68.99rem) {
    h1 {
      max-width: calc(100% - 56px);
    }
  }

  @media (min-width: 69rem) {
    .hero {
      flex-direction: row;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: space-between;
      gap: 24px;
      min-height: 780px;
      padding: 72px;
    }

    .hero-copy {
      flex: 0 1 580px;
    }

    .hero-art {
      flex: 0 0 auto;
      width: clamp(400px, 37.5vw, 540px);
      aspect-ratio: 540 / 700;
      margin: 0;
      --cut-top: 16.48cqi;
    }
  }

  /* ─── The other apps, struck through ───────────────────────────────────── */

  /*
    The lines between the hero and the Pilke section, on the page's own ground. Set
    in the display face at the size of a section head's second rank, left-aligned
    with a card's content, and with a card's padding above and below (`slab-open`),
    so the lines stand alone between the hero and that heading instead of reading as
    either one's caption.
    The words wrap freely on a phone; each step keeps its own strike.
  */
  /* The inline padding is `.section-pad`'s, so the first word stands where a card's
     content does. */
  .elsewhere > .wrap {
    max-width: none;
    padding-inline: var(--card-pad-inline);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 2.67vw + 1.35rem, 3.75rem);
    line-height: 1.2;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--text-soft);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  /* The English headline's stressed word, underlined in the strikes' coral. */
  .hero-mark {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-color: var(--brand-main);
    text-decoration-thickness: 0.09em;
    text-underline-offset: 0.14em;
    text-decoration-skip-ink: none;
  }

  /* The strike is a bar drawn over each step rather than `line-through`, so it can
     be drawn in: a little below the middle of the lowercase, in coral. */
  .elsewhere del {
    position: relative;
    display: inline-block;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .elsewhere del::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: -0.04em;
    right: -0.04em;
    top: 54%;
    height: 0.09em;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: var(--brand-main);
    transform-origin: left center;
  }

  /* Where the browser can tie an animation to scrolling and the reader has not
     asked for less motion, each strike is drawn as its step comes into view, one
     after the other; everywhere else they are simply there. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    @supports (animation-timeline: view()) {
      .elsewhere del::after {
        animation: strike linear both;
        animation-timeline: view();
        animation-range: entry 60% cover 40%;
      }

      .elsewhere del:nth-of-type(2)::after { animation-range: entry 70% cover 42%; }
      .elsewhere del:nth-of-type(3)::after { animation-range: entry 80% cover 44%; }
      .elsewhere del:nth-of-type(4)::after { animation-range: entry 90% cover 46%; }
      .elsewhere del:nth-of-type(5)::after { animation-range: entry 100% cover 48%; }
      .elsewhere del:nth-of-type(6)::after { animation-range: entry 100% cover 50%; }
    }
  }

  @keyframes strike {
    from {
      transform: scaleX(0);
    }

    to {
      transform: scaleX(1);
    }
  }

  .visually-hidden {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip-path: inset(50%);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* ─── What Pilke does instead, and how it goes ───────────────────────── */

  /* No padding above: the struck lines' own padding under them is the whole of the
     air between the two, so the heading reads as their answer. */
  .how > .wrap {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: clamp(16px, 0.76vw + 13px, 24px);
    padding-top: 0;
  }

  /* The heading and its lead centred over the four cards, the owner's call: they
     head the cards rather than continue the struck lines, so they stand on the
     cards' axis. */
  .how-head {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 16px;
    margin-bottom: clamp(16px, 1.52vw + 10px, 32px);
    padding-inline: var(--card-pad-inline);
    text-align: center;
  }

  .how-head h2 {
    max-width: 22ch;
  }

  /*
    The four steps. Stacked cards on a phone, each with its phone in a well under the
    copy; one wide card per row from 40rem, with the phone to the right; two by two
    from 75rem, where the row is wide enough for two of those.
  */
  .steps {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 16px;
  }

  .steps li {
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    min-height: 600px;
    padding: 28px 24px 0;
    border-radius: clamp(32px, 0.76vw + 29px, 40px);
  }

  .step-copy {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 14px;
  }

  .steps h3 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: clamp(1.875rem, 0.57vw + 1.74rem, 2.25rem);
    line-height: 1.05;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    hyphens: manual;
  }

  .step-copy p {
    font-size: clamp(1rem, 0.1vw + 0.98rem, 1.0625rem);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .step-phone {
    position: relative;
    flex: 1 0 250px;
    --phone-width: 210px;
  }

  .phone-hold {
    position: absolute;
    left: 50%;
    top: 36px;
    width: var(--phone-width);
    margin-left: calc(var(--phone-width) / -2);
  }

  /* The site's tag, tilted over the phone's top right corner, clear of the screen's
     title and its questions. */
  .soon {
    position: absolute;
    top: -12px;
    right: -14px;
    z-index: 1;
    transform: rotate(7deg);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
  }

  @media (min-width: 40rem) {
